  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1106 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nOn Saturday, a compact shortwave disturbance will quickly dive \nsoutheastward over the southern Rockies, inducing rapid lee \ncyclogenesis over NE NM and SE CO where a ~995mb surface low is \nprogged to develop by mid-afternoon. This will result in a dramatic \nstrengthening of westerly surface flow over much of the region on \nSaturday afternoon, with sustained winds possibly exceeding 30 mph \nespecially over the SW TX Panhandle. Temperatures will also jump \ninto at least the low 70s resulting in RH values in the upper teens \nto mid 20s, which could also bring localized elevated fire weather \nconditions to areas on the Caprock. The surface low will then begin \npulling eastward after sunset, which will send a strong cold front \nsouthward through the region on Saturday night. Surface pressure \nrises will be on the order of 9 to 12 mb in three hours, which \nsuggests that winds could be even stronger on Saturday night into \nearly Sunday compared to during the day Saturday with gusts up to \nabout 50 mph looking likely especially off the Caprock late Saturday \nnight. Luckily, these stronger winds will be relatively short-lived \nas surface ridging quickly builds overhead, but Sunday will be \nnotably cooler with highs likely to remain in the 40s to low 50s \narea-wide. Warmer weather then makes a comeback Monday through the \nmiddle of next week as persistent lee surface troughing re-\nestablishes to our northwest. Another disturbance may bring stronger \nwinds on Wednesday before a possible cooldown late next week, but \nprecipitation chances still remain near zero throughout the extended \nforecast.",
